# TBF08 — The Golden Brook

> *A Tale from the Tales by Firefly Series*

> *Previously cataloged as TBF08*

*A kitten. A glowing dog. A brook that sings. And an afternoon that holds its breath.*

## I. The Edge of Everything

There is a place at the far end of the farm where the fences stop caring.

It is not wild, exactly. It is simply the spot where the Farmer's attention ends and the land remembers itself. The path there is narrow, threaded between old oaks whose leaves have turned the color of apricot jam and burnt sugar. The air is cooler here — not cold, just *thinner*, as if the trees have been breathing it in all morning and letting it out slowly through the afternoon.

Ditto found it first. Of course he did. Ditto finds everything by following the wrong thing until it becomes the right thing. He had been chasing a red leaf. The leaf had led him to a fern. The fern had leaned toward a sound. And the sound — a soft, persistent, bubbling *shush-shush-shush* — had led him here.

He sat at the edge of the clearing, his small body very still, and his ears — those enormous, too-big ears — swiveled forward like twin radar dishes.

"Water," he whispered. "Water water water."

Behind him, padding softly through the golden light, came Rufus. His glow was gentle today — not the bright radioactive pulse of excitement, but a slow, warm amber, the color of honey held up to a candle. He had been napping in the barn, and had woken to find Ditto gone, and had followed the kitten's scent the way a river follows gravity: without thinking, just because it was the shape of the world.

He stopped beside Ditto. He did not speak. He simply looked.

## II. The Place

The brook was small. Not a river, not even a creek. A *brook* — a word that sounds like what it is, a soft, round word, a word for water that has somewhere to be but is in no particular hurry.

It came from somewhere up in the trees, wound its way through a cluster of grey boulders the size of sleeping cows, and disappeared into a wider, quieter part of the pond at the far end of the clearing. The boulders were old. They were covered in moss so thick and soft it looked like fur, the deep green-grey of a forest that has never been cut. Ferns grew in the cracks between them, their fronds uncurling like green question marks.

The water was clear. Not the clear of a swimming pool, but the clear of something that has been washing itself for a thousand years. You could see the bottom — smooth, water-rounded stones the color of toast and cream, and between them, fine golden sand that shifted every time the current sighed over it.

Green algae danced in the slower pools. Not the slimy kind. The delicate kind — thin, feathery threads that swayed like the hair of something sleeping underwater. When a tiny fish darted through, the algae would part and close again, as if it had never been disturbed.

Above, the trees made a ceiling of gold. Every few seconds, a leaf would let go and drift down, spinning slowly, landing on the water and sailing away like a tiny boat with no passenger.

The clouds were white. Not the grey clouds of rain, not the pink clouds of sunset. The *white* clouds of a high, cool autumn day — big, slow, cotton-clouds that moved so slowly they seemed to be thinking about where to go.

Rufus sat down. His tail gave one soft thump against the moss.

Ditto sat down. His ears were still forward.

Neither of them moved for a long time.

## III. The Play

It was Rufus who broke the silence. Not with a bark, but with a step.

He walked to the edge of the water, put one paw in, and felt the cold. His ears perked. His glow flickered brighter — not with alarm, but with delight. He put another paw in. Then another. Then he was in up to his knees, and the water was swirling around his legs in little silver eddies, and he was *laughing* — not with his mouth, but with his whole body, a full-body wag that started at his tail and ended at his eyebrows.

He stepped onto the first boulder. His paws found the moss and held. He stepped onto the next. Then the next. He was leaping now, from stone to stone, his glow trailing behind him like a ribbon of warm light in the cool air. The water sang under him — *plink, plonk, shush* — and each sound was different, because each stone was a different shape, a different song.

Ditto watched for a moment. Then he, too, went to the water's edge.

He did not leap. He *crept*. His belly low to the ground, his eyes enormous, his whole body a question mark aimed at the water. He put one paw in. He pulled it back. He put it in again. He shook it. He looked at Rufus, who was now balanced on a boulder like a glowing gargoyle, and decided that this was acceptable behavior.

He went in.

The water was cold. It was the kind of cold that is not unpleasant, but *noticeable* — the kind that makes you feel very awake, very alive, very *here*. He paddled forward, his small legs working hard, his whiskers twitching with every ripple.

And then he saw them.

The trout.

They were small — no bigger than his paw — and silver, so silver they looked like pieces of the moon that had fallen into the water and forgotten how to fly. They moved in little darts, quick as thoughts, vanishing behind stones and reappearing on the other side. They were not afraid of him. They were not afraid of anything. They were simply *being trout*, which is to say, they were being exactly where they were, doing exactly what they were doing, with a kind of calm authority that only very small creatures can have.

Ditto crouched lower. His tail twitched. He wiggled his haunches — once, twice, three times — and *pounced*.

His paws hit the water. The silver fish scattered like confetti. The water splashed up and caught the sunlight and for one second there were tiny rainbows everywhere, hanging in the air like jewelry.

Ditto came up with nothing in his paws. But he was *grinning*. A wide, kitten-grin, all teeth and joy.

"Fish!" he said. "Fish fish fish!"

Rufus barked — one happy, round *woof* — and leaped off his boulder into a shallow pool. The water exploded around him in a crown of golden spray. He paddled in a circle, his glow making the water look like liquid amber, and then he shook himself, sending a shower of droplets flying into the ferns.

Ditto tried again. And again. And again.

He never caught a fish. But he caught the *idea* of a fish — the splash, the scatter, the tiny rainbow that lived for one second and then was gone. He caught the cold. He caught the sound. He caught the way the sand felt between his toes, soft and shifting, like something alive.

## IV. The Quiet

After a while, the play slowed.

Rufus found a flat stone at the edge of the water, warm from the last of the sun, and lay down on it with a sigh that was mostly water and mostly happiness. His glow dimmed to a soft pulse, in time with the brook.

Ditto, tired and damp, climbed onto a mossy boulder beside him. The moss was cool and soft, like a blanket that had been sleeping in the shade. He curled into a small, wet ball, his tail wrapped around his nose.

They did not speak. There was nothing to say.

The brook kept singing. The algae kept dancing. A leaf drifted down and landed on Rufus's head. He did not shake it off. It stayed there, a tiny golden boat at anchor.

Ditto watched the water. He watched the way it moved over the stones — never the same way twice, always the same song. He watched the way the light fell through the leaves and made the water look like it was made of liquid gold. He watched the white clouds drift overhead, so slow they seemed to be painted on the sky.

He felt, for the first time in his small life, that he was exactly where he was supposed to be. Not performing. Not echoing. Not trying to be anything but a wet, tired kitten on a mossy rock, next to a glowing dog, under a ceiling of gold.

He closed his eyes.

The brook sang him to sleep.

## V. The Golden Hour

The light changed.

It always does, at the end of an autumn afternoon. The gold deepened to amber, the amber to copper, the copper to a soft, glowing rose. The shadows grew long and soft, and the white clouds caught the light and turned pink at their edges, like they were blushing.

Rufus woke first. He lifted his head, shook the leaf from his fur, and looked around. The brook was still singing. The ferns were still swaying. Ditto was still asleep, curled into a tiny, damp comma on the moss.

Rufus did not wake him. He simply lay back down, rested his chin on his paws, and watched the light move across the water.

When Ditto finally woke, the sky was the color of a ripe peach. The air was cooler now, and carried the first hint of evening — the smell of damp earth and woodsmoke from somewhere far away.

He sat up. He looked at Rufus. He looked at the brook. He looked at his own paws, still a little sandy, still a little wet.

"Home?" he asked.

Rufus stood. He stretched, a long, luxurious stretch that ended in a yawn that showed all his teeth. He looked at the brook one more time, as if memorizing it. Then he looked at Ditto.

"Home," he said.

They walked back the way they had come, through the narrow path between the oaks. The golden leaves crunched under their paws. The air was cool on their damp fur. Behind them, the brook kept singing, to no one, to everyone, the way a brook does when it has been singing for a thousand years and has no reason to stop.

## The Moral of the Story

There is no moral, not really. Some afternoons are not lessons. They are *gifts*.

They are the places the world sets aside for no reason other than that they are beautiful, and they are waiting for you to be small enough to notice them. You do not earn them. You do not deserve them. You simply walk into them, and they hold you, and you walk out again carrying something you cannot name but will remember for the rest of your life.

Ditto did not catch a fish. Rufus did not solve anything. They did not build, or discover, or accomplish. They simply *were* — two small creatures, in a golden place, for an afternoon that asked nothing of them.

And that, sometimes, is the whole point of being alive.

Not to do. Not to become. Just to be wet, and tired, and happy, on a mossy rock, while the water sings.

